## Filtering Optional Values

`.filter()` keeps the value if a predicate is true, and returns `None` if the predicate is false (or if it was already `None`).

### Basic filter

```xiom
let a = Some(10).filter(|x| x > 5);  // Some(10) — 10 > 5 is true
let b = Some(3).filter(|x| x > 5);   // None — 3 > 5 is false
let c: Option[Int] = None;
let d = c.filter(|x| x > 5);          // None — already None
```

### filter for Validation

```xiom
fn positive(n: Int) -> Option[Int] {
  return Some(n).filter(|x| x > 0);
}

let a = positive(5);   // Some(5)
let b = positive(-3);  // None
let c = positive(0);   // None — 0 is not > 0
```

### Combining filter with map

```xiom
fn parse_and_validate(s: Str) -> Option[Int] {
  // Assume parsing returns Some(n)
  return Some(42)
    .filter(|n| n % 2 == 0)  // only evens
    .map(|n| n * 2);         // double it
}
```

### filter with Strings

```xiom
fn valid_username(name: Str) -> Option[Str] {
  return Some(name)
    .filter(|n| n.len() >= 3)
    .filter(|n| n.len() <= 20);
}

let ok = valid_username("Alice");      // Some("Alice")
let bad = valid_username("ab");        // None
```

### Pipe-like Pattern

```xiom
let score = Some(85)
  .filter(|s| s >= 0)
  .filter(|s| s <= 100)
  .unwrap_or(0);  // 85 — passes all filters
```

